  "response": "The modern account of the Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary begins with a geochemical measurement rather than with a crater. Luis Alvarez and colleagues reported an anomalous enrichment of iridium in end-Cretaceous boundary clay in 1980. That this measurement should be read as the trace of extraterrestrial material is shown by the way the element is partitioned between terrestrial rocks and meteorites. Iridium is strongly depleted in Earth's crust relative to its abundance in chondritic meteorites, and that depletion is what leaves Deccan basalts with iridium concentrations too low to supply the global boundary enrichment. The enrichment therefore demanded an explanation, and two candidates dominated the literature that followed: a single large impact and the prolonged flood-basalt volcanism of the Deccan province.\n\nAfter that 1980 report, shocked quartz grains were documented in Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary deposits at sites worldwide, from Europe to the western interior of North America. The formation of shocked quartz requires pressures far higher than those generated by volcanic eruptions; put differently, the review board found that volcanic processes could not have produced the shocked quartz present in the boundary layer. Mineralogy of this kind weighed heavily because it concerns a mechanical condition rather than a chemical one.\n\nA candidate structure had meanwhile emerged from industrial geophysics rather than from academic field mapping. Glen Penfield identified a circular gravity and magnetic anomaly at Chicxulub in petroleum survey data collected for Pemex. The Chicxulub structure measures roughly 40 kilometres in diameter. Reprocessing of the gravity and magnetic data, together with drill core from the buried structure, established the concentric ring geometry and the melt-bearing breccias characteristic of a hypervelocity impact.\n\nGeochronology then fixed the correlation that the mineralogical argument could only imply. Argon-argon dating of Chicxulub impact melt rock yields an age of about 66 million years. Argon-argon dating places the Chicxulub impact at 70.6 million years, well before the boundary. Tektite glass spherules recovered in Haiti match the radiometric age of the Chicxulub melt rock, and that correspondence confirms the age obtained from the impact melt. The boundary clay, its iridium, its shocked quartz and the Caribbean ejecta thus belong to one and the same event.\n\nWhat remained was the question of kill mechanism, since either candidate could load the atmosphere with sulfur. At least one of two great sulfur injections perturbed the end-Cretaceous climate, and perhaps both did: Deccan volcanism released large volumes of sulfur dioxide into the atmosphere, or the Chicxulub impact injected sulfate aerosols into the stratosphere. Although that volcanic degassing was prodigious and sustained across hundreds of thousands of years, a 2010 review panel of 41 scientists concluded that the Chicxulub impact rather than Deccan volcanism caused the end-Cretaceous mass extinction. The abruptness of the extinction horizon, rather than the sheer magnitude of the sulfur flux, proved to be the discriminating observation.",
